      "percussionGap": 8,
      "notes": "PRESTIGE PASS (\u00a79.6). Half-time at 100 BPM: 16 rows to the bar, 4 bars to a frame, 1 row = 150 ms. AXIS 11 EVIDENCE. (a) Tresillo: frame 2 re-groups the riff's own pitches as 3+3+2 at the 16th level - attacks at frame 2 : rows 0,3,6 | 8,11,14 | 16,19,22 | 24,27,30, and the same grouping across bars 3-4 ending at row 62; the 3-row unit is 450 ms and the group heads (rows 0,8,16,24,32,40,48,56) carry the accent so it reads as grouping, not as noise. (b) Displacement: frame 4 plays the identical riff shifted +2 rows (frame 4 : row 2 is what frame 1 : row 0 was) against an unmoved kit and bass - the collision IS the effect; the closing note is shortened to a release so nothing crosses row 63. (c) Metric surprise, exactly one: D00 at frame 6 : row 59, so the last B frame runs 60 rows and the break arrives a beat early. It sits on a NEW noise pattern (index 8) because frames 5 and 6 otherwise share every lane's index. AXIS 4 EVIDENCE. pulse 2 is an independent line, not the lead at an interval: across frames 1-4 it attacks on the 4-row grid (rows 0,4,8,12,16,20,26,28,32,36,40,44,48,52,56,60) against the lead's syncopated set, which puts 62% of its attacks where the lead has none, and it moves contrary to the lead on every shared attack row (frame 1 : rows 16, 20, 32, 48, 56). A'' gets a different counter-line per frame (pulse2 p3 at frame 9, p5 at frame 10, p4 at frame 11), never below 43% non-coincident. WRITTEN DISSONANCES, two. (1) A prepared suspension: pulse 2 attacks b3 at frame 11 : row 28 over Em, where b is the fifth - the bass no longer anticipates F on that row (frame 11 : row 28 is e2, note 40), so the harmony turns to F major cleanly at frame 11 : row 32 with the b3 held through the change as a suspended flat-5, and it resolves DOWN BY STEP to a3 at frame 11 : row 36. (2) A flat-9 suspension at the final cadence: pulse 2 attacks c4 (note 60) at frame 11 : row 44 as the fifth of the F bar, holds it through the change to B7 at frame 11 : row 48 where it is the flat ninth, and resolves down by step to b3 at frame 11 : row 50. NON-DIATONIC DEVICES, two distinct, in different sections: (1) modal interchange - the phrygian bII, F major, arriving on a downbeat at frame 9 : row 32 (f natural = note 65 in the lead, 41 in the bass) and closing F->Em, a phrygian cadence; (2) secondary dominant - B7 at frame 11 : row 48, its raised third d sharp at frame 11 : row 52 in the lead (75) and pulse 2 (63), the bass reaching note 39 at frame 11 : row 56, resolving to e across the loop seam. A'S PEDAL IS INTENTIONAL: the A section is a riff over a sustained E-minor pedal, a deliberate one-chord vamp, because what A is about is the riff's own tresillo re-grouping and its +2 displacement, and because the harmonic argument is saved for the phrygian bII in B and A''. It is not motionless: the bass leans to Am, the iv, on the last half-beat of bar 3 - note 45 at frame 1 : row 46 - so every A frame closes iv - V (b1 at frame 1 : row 48) - i instead of sitting on E for sixteen bars. KIT LANGUAGE. Ghost snares at vol 4-6 on the off-16ths between the backbeats: rows 11, 15, 27, 31, 43, 47, 59, 63. Two or more kit elements change per section: A = 8th hats + snare 39; B (frames 5-6) = hats on off-8ths only + a metal(44, mode 1) tick on every bar downbeat; break (frames 7-8) = hats out entirely, ghosts only, so the DPCM pump is naked; A'' (frames 9-11) = 16ths in the hat lane through bar 1 (rows 1-15), snare moved to note 41, metal ticks kept. Five distinct fills, no two alike: snare roll (frame 2 : 48-60), toms with pit-tom-drop (frame 4 : 56-62), all-ghost (frame 6 : 52-59), crash-led (frame 8 : 56-62), pitch-macro riser on x-rust-and-neon-riser (frame 10 : 56-62 - a noise note at index 10 whose pitch macro walks the index DOWN to 2 over 8 ticks, which on this channel is a rising sweep). The hat lane's 16ths are confined to bar 1 of frame 9 to stay under \u00a79.8's ceiling of 28 attacks per bar across all five lanes; two bars of 16ths would have put frame 9 at 31. UNCHANGED IDENTITY: e minor, tempo 150 / speed 9 = 100 BPM, 12 frames, loopFrame 1, DPCM carries kick(36) + snare(39) and there is no noise kick anywhere; the triangle still re-strikes on rows 0/8/16/24 of every bar so the shared TND index ducks the bass with the beat. DECIMAL PARAMS: 7 param 52 = 0x34 = tremolo speed 3 depth 4; S param 7 cuts a 9-tick row after 7 ticks; G param 1-2 = a 1-2 tick nudge, never on a section downbeat; V param 1 = noise short mode, 0 = long; A param 32 = 0x20 = Ax0 = fade DOWN (the direction is inverted here), cancelled with A00; D param 0 = enter the next frame at row 0. The intro's silent noise lane is the only over-long percussion gap (64 of 764 rows, 8.4%), inside Gate B's 20% slack. The suite-wide rmsRange convention: the measured two-pass mix RMS +-1.5 dB, declared per song because the design's [-20,-9] window is unreachable on this engine and the floor is -30 dBFS. Measured -23.30 dBFS, declared [-24.8, -21.8].",
